WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.340
Welcome to Environmental Intelligence, episode 23 for April 13th, 2026, new week.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.600
Here's what's changed across Canadian environmental jurisdictions

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:14.700
and why it matters for your files this quarter. Ontario has moved forward with 14 new renewable

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.580
electricity procurement contracts, while Arsler Middle, Dofasco permanently shuts its last

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:25.040
coke oven battery in Hamilton today. These two developments, one on the supply side and one on the

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.020
industrial emissions side, are reshaping how consultants build decarbonization roadmaps,

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:35.440
update air compliance models, and advise on electricity attributes in corporate GHG

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.380
inventories. Ontario's decision to contract 14 renewable energy projects is more than another

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:47.180
procurement round. It forms part of the province's long-term strategy to displace higher emitting generation,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:53.020
secure capacity under the Electricity Act, and give large emitters a credible path to lower scope

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:57.380
to emissions, without waiting for the federal carbon price to do all the heavy lifting.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.120
For environmental professionals supporting proponents through impact assessment

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:08.280
act processes, these contracts directly change the baseline grid emission factor that must be used

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.020
in cumulative effects assessments and in the electricity supply component of project descriptions.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:18.420
Practitioners working on contaminated sites or industrial facilities should now evaluate whether

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.980
these new power purchase agreements can be folded into corporate renewable energy attribute

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:29.160
tracking, Ontario emissions reporting, or even site specific decarbonization plans that regulators

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:34.720
will scrutinize during approval renewals. The practical next step is to pull the eventual contract

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.260
award details and grid connection timelines into client compliance roadmaps before the end of Q2.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.860
What this signals is a concrete measurable shift in how electricity supply will be treated in

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:51.460
provincial emissions inventories and in future IA submissions. Teams that update their forecasting

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:57.180
models now will avoid having to retrofit scope two numbers six months from now when regulators start

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:02.340
asking harder questions. The story in Hamilton is equally material. Today, April 13th,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.440
Arsler Middle, DeFasco will push the last Coke from one of its two remaining Coke plants as part of a

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.000
multi-year decarbonization pathway that has been under discussion with the Ministry of the Environment,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.700
Conservation, and Parks for some time. The permanent shutdown reduces Coke oven gas production

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.100
and the associated suite of air emissions that have been managed under site specific Ontario EPA

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:29.040
approvals and waste management authorizations. For consultants supporting either the facility itself

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.560
or nearby contaminated sites, the change in source term is not trivial. Air quality dispersion

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.040
modeling, odor complaint response plans, and even groundwater monitoring programs that have

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.060
historically captured deposition of co-related constituents will need to be updated to reflect the

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:49.380
new baseline. The company has publicly indicated that this is only one milestone on the road to full

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:55.600
decarbonization, which suggests further changes to both air and water discharge profiles are likely

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.560
in the next 24 to 36 months. Practitioners should therefore treat today's event as a trigger to

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:08.000
revisit conceptual site models for any Hamilton area projects, confirm updated emission factors with

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.520
the company's environmental group and adjust long-term compliance forecasts accordingly.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.600
Sites that sit down gradient or downwind will feel the difference first. Getting ahead of the revised

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.860
source terms now prevents awkward conversations during the next round of annual reporting or EA

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:29.420
follow-up. Moving from provincial decarbonization signals to the federal and international stage,

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:35.300
recent leadership changes at the US EPA and continued debate over biomass carbon accounting are

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:40.840
creating noticeable ripples across the border. The new EPA direction under Lee Zeldin has re-oriented

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:46.060
messaging toward industry support, expanded fossil fuel exports, and a lighter touch approach to

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:51.660
traditional environmental enforcement. For Canadian practitioners who manage cross-border contaminated

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:58.300
sites, particularly those in the Great Lakes basin, this shift matters. Bilateral initiatives

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:03.160
under the Great Lakes water quality agreement, joint remediation projects, and expectations for

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:08.720
harmonized standards that have historically been anchored in CEPA and bilateral data sharing protocols

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.400
could slow or change character. Timelines for sediment remediation, groundwater plume delineation

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.880
that crosses the border, and even the frequency of joint monitoring may be revisited.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:24.860
Canadian teams should therefore track US policy statements over the next several weeks and assess whether

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:31.460
project-specific expectations for data quality, risk assessment endpoints, or remedial verification need

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:36.840
to be buffered. The practical takeaways to review any active or planned Great Lakes files this month,

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:43.000
and flag where reliance on near-term US regulatory alignment may no longer be prudent. At the same time,

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:48.420
biomass carbon accounting debates continue to intensify. A recent life cycle analysis makes a

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:53.260
compelling case that wood pellet combustion offers far less climate benefit than previously assumed

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.700
once foregone forest sequestration and full supply chain emissions are counted. This directly

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:02.460
challenges the carbon neutrality assumption still embedded in several provincial offset protocols

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:08.520
and in parts of the federal offset system. For practitioners validating renewable energy attributes,

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:15.000
preparing corporate GHG inventories under BC's carbon pricing regime or supporting clients seeking

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.680
offset credits, the implication is clear. Substitution factors and baseline assumptions that were

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:26.160
acceptable 12 months ago may now attract regulatory or third-party scrutiny. The prudent move is to

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:32.120
revisit biomass-related lines in current client portfolios, stress-test the accounting methodology

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:37.420
against the latest life cycle literature, and prepare briefing notes for industrial clients who still

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:42.600
rely heavily on biomass and their decarbonization narratives. Those who adjust early will be better

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:47.780
positioned when federal and provincial carbon pricing reviews accelerate in the second half of

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:54.020
2026. Another international development worth contextualizing for Canadian practice is Ireland's

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:59.000
recent decision following widespread protests to cut petrol and diesel prices by 10 cents per

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:06.220
liter and defer a planned carbon tax increase inside a 505 million euros relief package. While the

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:11.280
political context is European, the precedent is relevant for anyone modeling Canadian client exposure

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.320
under the greenhouse gas pollution pricing act and parallel provincial systems. Rapid price adjustments

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:22.520
or deferrals create political sensitivity that federal and provincial governments cannot entirely ignore.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:28.160
Practitioners advising on carbon compliance offset markets or corporate climate risk should

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.860
therefore incorporate higher volatility scenarios into their forecasting models and flag the potential

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:39.280
for policy lag or mid-course corrections when presenting exposure numbers to boards or lenders.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:45.320
The Irish example is a useful reminder that social license remains a material variable even in

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:51.200
mature carbon pricing jurisdictions. On the scientific front, a study published in environmental

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:58.200
research used cancer registry data and detailed facility locations to identify statistically significant

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:03.800
associations between proximity to large livestock feeding operations and elevated cancer incidents in

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:10.740
parts of California, Iowa and Texas. The analysis points to bioerisals, nitrates and volatile organic

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:16.280
compounds as plausible cumulative stressors. For Canadian practitioners conducting human health

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:21.600
risk assessments near intensive agricultural operations, the findings reinforce the limitations

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:30.220
of relying solely on CCME soil and groundwater pathways under BCCSR, Alberta EPA or Ontario.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:39.260
OREG 153-004, risk assessors should now give serious consideration to airborne exposure routes,

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:45.040
nitrate loading to shallow groundwater, and the additive effects of multiple agricultural stressors

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:48.760
when setting site specific standards or developing risk management plans.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.260
The study is a prompt to expand conceptual site models beyond the classic contaminant list

00:07:54.260 --> 00:08:00.260
and to ensure that toxicology reviews capture the latest literature on chronic low-level exposures.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:06.440
Similarly, the first detection of medium-chain chlorinated paraffins, MCCPs,

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:11.900
in North American air samples, with sewage sludge-derived fertilizer identified as the likely source,

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:18.140
establishes a previously unquantified atmospheric transport pathway for these persistent compounds.

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:26.920
Canadian laboratories accredited to ISO-17025 should anticipate growing demand for MCCP analysis in both

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:32.880
air and soil matrices, particularly at sites located near historical or current biosolids

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:38.560
application under provincial contaminated sites regimes. Risk assessors will likely need to

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:43.720
expand their conceptual site models to include atmospheric deposition and subsequent leaching

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:49.560
as a plausible complete pathway. The practical implication is to begin conversations now

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:55.920
with accredited labs about method development, detection limits, and QAQC protocols so that analytical

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.860
capacity is in place before regulators start asking for these parameters in risk assessment reports.

