00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:34.720
Table two standard and one overburden well while the adjacent well only eight meters away

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:39.180
reports non-detect, despite clear evidence of an upgradient source.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:44.560
What you are observing is not sampling error, but the signature of discontinuous silt lenses,

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:50.680
sand stringers, and diamecton facies that create preferential pathways and isolated low permeability

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:57.940
pockets. Under BCCR protocol four or CCME tier two risk assessment, the regulatory expectation is

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:03.640
that this heterogeneity must be characterized at the facies scale rather than masked by two-dimensional

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:09.100
contouring. Experience teams therefore supplement conventional monitoring wells with drive point

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:14.800
profilers, continuous core logging, and high-resolution vertical profiling to map hydraulic conductivity

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:20.080
transitions at the centimeter scale. The 20-year practitioner quickly learns that apparent hot spots

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.640
often align with sand lenses that have already flushed, while the real mass remains trapped in the low

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:31.540
K matrix that will sustain back diffusion for decades. The most common and costly mistake is assuming

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:36.560
uniform plume geometry when preparing a risk assessment or designing a permeable reactive barrier.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:43.020
The fix is straightforward. Mandate at least one continuous core and one multi-level sampler per 100

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:48.580
square meters of suspected source zone before finalizing the conceptual site model or selecting a

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:54.360
remedy. Teams that invest in this resolution early reduce the likelihood of unpleasant surprises

00:10:54.360 --> 00:11:00.320
during regulatory review or long-term performance monitoring. So pulling the week together for your

